/**
 * @defgroup defer Defer Statements
 * @ingroup module_development
 * @brief Block-scoped cleanup statements for C, Objective-C, and C++
 */

/**
 * @page topic_defer Defer Statements
 * @ingroup defer
 *
 * Include `<ascii-chat/tooling/defer/defer.h>` and place `defer(action);` in a
 * compound statement. The action runs when execution leaves that block. A
 * block action can be written as `defer({ statements; });`.
 * C++11 and later, including Objective-C++, use a native scope guard rather than build-time rewriting.
 * Its cleanup runs on exception unwinding as well as normal block exit;
 * cleanup must not throw because the guard destructor is `noexcept`.
 * Both backends accept C++ source files and preserve them for native C++
 * compilation. C++ scopes follow the language's normal object lifetime rules.
 * On Windows, including the defer header makes Clang reject Microsoft-extension
 * goto jumps that bypass a local variable's initialization, because bypassing
 * a defer guard would leave its cleanup state uninitialized.
 * Objective-C (`.m`) is transformed using `@try`/`@finally`, so registered
 * actions also run while Objective-C exceptions unwind. This works in C
 * functions, Objective-C methods, and block literals.
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * FILE *file = fopen(path, "r");
 * if (!file) return ERROR;
 * defer(fclose(file));
 * // Use file; fclose runs when this block exits.
 * @endcode
 *
 * `SAFE_MALLOC_DEFER_FREE(name, size, pointer_type)` and
 * `SAFE_CALLOC_DEFER_FREE(name, count, size, pointer_type)` declare a tracked
 * allocation and register `SAFE_FREE(name)` for the enclosing block. Include
 * `<ascii-chat/common.h>` as well as the defer header. Like other deferred
 * actions, cleanup reads the current pointer value at exit; after transferring
 * ownership, set the local pointer to `NULL`.
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * SAFE_CALLOC_DEFER_FREE(items, count, sizeof(*items), item_t *);
 * if (!populate(items, count)) return ERROR;
 * @endcode
 *
 * In C, ordinary macros may wrap `defer()` through multiple levels. The transformer
 * expands those wrappers before analyzing cleanup. Wrapper definitions that
 * use stringification, token pasting, `__VA_OPT__`, `_Pragma`, or `__COUNTER__`
 * are rejected, as are multiline invocations and arguments containing non-defer
 * macros (use a local value instead). The expanded defer must still be a direct
 * statement in a block. CMake discovers wrappers declared in project headers;
 * set `ASCIICHAT_DEFER_WRAPPER_HEADER_DIRS` to a semicolon-separated list of
 * additional header directories when wrappers are declared outside the project.
 *
 * Reaching a defer registers it. Actions run in reverse registration order,
 * with inner blocks cleaned up first. They evaluate their expressions at exit,
 * using the current values of referenced variables. They run on fallthrough,
 * return, break, continue, and goto that leaves the block. A return expression
 * is evaluated and converted to the function return type before cleanup.
 * Nonlocal transfers such as longjmp and process
 * termination do not run deferred actions.
 *
 * `defer_named(handle, action);` registers a cancellable action, and
 * `defer_cancel(handle);` cancels it. The handle is a lexical name, not a C
 * variable: it cannot be copied, stored, or passed to another function. It is
 * visible after registration in its block and nested blocks. Names of active
 * handles must be unique. Canceling an already canceled handle is harmless;
 * leaving and re-entering a loop body registers a fresh action.
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * defer_named(release_buffer, free(buffer));
 * if (hand_off(buffer) == SUCCESS)
 *   defer_cancel(release_buffer);
 * @endcode
 *
 * Defer is supported in project `.c` and `.m` files and supported inline
 * function bodies in included headers.
 * Each defer must be a direct statement of a `{ ... }` block. Add braces around
 * an if or loop body before using defer there. Put a defer inside braces under a
 * switch case; a defer directly in a switch body is rejected. Inline assembly
 * in a function containing defer is rejected because its control flow may be
 * opaque, including `asm goto`. Computed goto,
 * jumps into a block containing defer, backward jumps that can register the same defer repeatedly, and
 * unnamed non-builtin, non-pointer or variably modified parameters in non-void defer functions
 * are rejected. Cleanup actions may not contain return,
 * goto, break, continue, another defer, or `__COUNTER__`. The transformer also
 * rejects source-level line directives, token-pasting macros in cleanup,
 * preprocessor changes that can alter cleanup, and exits where an action identifier may bind to a different local
 * declaration. These restrictions make unsupported
 * control flow a build error rather than silently losing cleanup.
 *
 * CMake builds and uses the C++ transformer by default. It writes generated
 * sources below `build/defer_transformed/`; original source files are unchanged.
 * The native tool uses LLVM support and libclang development libraries and does
 * not require Python. Set `ASCIICHAT_LLVM_DEV_ROOT` when the development files
 * cannot be found automatically. A cross build builds a separate host tool;
 * `ASCIICHAT_DEFER_NATIVE_BINARY` can instead point to a prebuilt host tool.
 * The native tool transforms batch files concurrently using available CPU
 * threads. Pass `--jobs=N` when invoking it directly to set a smaller limit.
 * During incremental native builds, the batch reuses transformations whose
 * source, recorded included files, and build context are unchanged; it reports
 * how many files it transformed. A file is reprocessed when its own compiler
 * arguments, dependencies, libclang version, or the tool binary change.
 * `ASCIICHAT_DEFER_SHARED_CACHE` optionally sets a shared cache directory,
 * such as `ASCIICHAT_DEPS_CACHE_ROOT/defer`. It can reuse results across build
 * directories for the same source tree, after checking dependency contents
 * and the current include graph. Files using time-dependent macros or
 * `__has_include` are transformed normally instead of being shared.
 * It is disabled by default because checking a cache can cost more than a
 * fresh transformation. Direct tool users can
 * select the cache with `--shared-cache` together with `--incremental`.
 * `--preamble` enables experimental Clang preamble reuse for direct tool
 * invocations; it is not enabled by CMake because measurements may be slower.
 * Each generated translation unit has its own directory, and the original
 * source directory remains on Clang's quoted-include search path. Relative
 * includes therefore resolve from the original source tree, including nested
 * includes; included files are tracked as build dependencies. Per-source
 * dependency stamps cause only affected objects to rebuild when an included
 * file changes, even if the transformed C text itself stays unchanged.
 * With Ninja, a lightweight eligibility scan runs on each build. Editing an
 * ordinary source does not reconfigure CMake; adding or removing its first
 * defer call changes the source set and automatically regenerates the build
 * graph before compilation. Unix Makefiles use conservative reconfiguration
 * on C source changes to ensure the same correctness.
 *
 * Set `ASCIICHAT_DEFER_BACKEND=PYTHON` to select the retained Python backend.
 * It runs through the defer uv project and requires uv-managed Python 3.10 or
 * newer and LLVM 19 or newer libclang; set
 * `ASCIICHAT_LIBCLANG_PATH` if its shared library cannot be found. The CMake
 * integration for either backend requires a compilation database and a
 * single-config Ninja or Unix Makefiles generator. Their generated C and
 * fail-closed behavior are checked together.
 * Directly compiling an untransformed C source containing defer fails rather
 * than turning cleanup into a no-op. C header functions use per-translation-
 * unit Clang VFS overlays. `ASCIICHAT_DEFER_HEADER_OVERLAYS=AUTO` detects
 * defer in project headers; `ON` forces this path and `OFF` disables it.
 * Add external header roots to `ASCIICHAT_DEFER_WRAPPER_HEADER_DIRS`, or use
 * `ON` when headers are outside the scanned roots. The first activation
 * analyzes all eligible project C and Objective-C sources and disables target precompiled
 * headers; later builds use native per-file incremental caching. The shared
 * cache is not used while header overlays are active. Multiple active
 * instantiations of the same header function in one translation unit are
 * rejected. Defer in an included C
 * source file remains unsupported. C++ uses native scope guards directly.
 * Objective-C and Objective-C++ require an Objective-C runtime advertising
 * zero-cost exceptions, with exception handling enabled. CMake adds
 * `-fobjc-exceptions` when transforming `.m` sources. Direct tool users must
 * pass it in the actual compilation arguments and compile with the same flags.
 * A CMake project compiling `.m` files must enable its `OBJC` language; the
 * ascii-chat project already enables it on Apple platforms. On Linux, provide
 * a modern GNUstep libobjc2 runtime, select it with an appropriate
 * `-fobjc-runtime=gnustep-2.0` compiler option, and link that runtime.
 * Apple exception unwinding has been verified; the legacy GNU Objective-C
 * runtime does not support this contract and is rejected. Objective-C jumps
 * into a generated protected region are rejected by Clang validation.
 * For C sources, panic instrumentation runs before defer transformation when
 * enabled. Objective-C sources are not instrumented by the panic tool.
 *
 * Both implementations can transform several files in one invocation using
 * `--batch manifest.txt`. Each UTF-8 manifest line contains an input path and
 * an output path separated by a tab. With `--compilation-database`, pass either
 * the directory containing `compile_commands.json` or the file itself. The
 * tool looks up the generated output first, then the original input; multiple
 * matching commands are rejected because the correct configuration is unclear.
 *
 * Without a compilation database, add a third tab-separated field containing
 * a JSON array of Clang arguments for that file. For example, a manifest can
 * contain the following lines, with each `<TAB>` replaced by an actual tab:
 * @code{.txt}
 * src/a.c<TAB>out/a.c<TAB>["-std=c17", "-DVALUE=1", "-Iinclude"]
 * src/b.c<TAB>out/b.c<TAB>["-std=c23", "-DVALUE=2", "-Iinclude"]
 * @endcode
 * The arrays contain parser arguments, not the compiler executable, input,
 * output, or shell-quoted command. Relative paths in those arguments resolve
 * from the tool's working directory; use `-working-directory` when needed.
 * A two-field row without a database uses no additional arguments. Explicit
 * per-file arguments and a compilation database cannot be combined. Native
 * batch mode supports `--dependency-stamps --incremental` with either form;
 * changing one file's arguments invalidates that file's cached transformation.
 *
 * For a single source, either implementation automatically reads redirected
 * stdin and writes validated transformed bytes to stdout when no input/output
 * paths or other modes are specified:
 * @code{.sh}
 * defer-tool --filename path/to/input.c -- -std=c17 -Iinclude < input.c > output.c
 * # Equivalent positional form:
 * defer-tool --filename path/to/input.c - - -- -std=c17 -Iinclude < input.c > output.c
 * @endcode
 * `--filename` is required for stdin input. It determines the input language,
 * source locations, and relative include lookup. It is a
 * virtual path and is not read or overwritten. A compilation database may be
 * used instead of explicit flags when it has an entry for that path. Diagnostics
 * go to stderr and invalid input produces no transformed output. This mode
 * handles one translation unit and does not support header overlays, dependency
 * files, or batch options. The ascii-chat CMake build always uses batch mode.
 *
 * To inspect an exit without writing a generated file, pass its source line and
 * the C compilation arguments to either implementation:
 * @code{.sh}
 * defer-tool --explain src/example.c:42 -- -std=c23 -Iinclude
 * @endcode
 * Actions are listed in cleanup order. Each runs only if execution actually
 * reached its registration and has not canceled it; the output does not claim
 * to predict branch choices.
 * Use explicit Clang arguments for this mode.
 * Use `defer-tool --show-transformed src/example.c:42 -- -std=c23 -Iinclude`
 * to print the generated version of the function containing that line. The
 * displayed snippet includes source mapping directives and is for inspection;
 * it is not a standalone translation unit.
 *
 * For a longer randomized check on a Unix host with Clang and libFuzzer,
 * configure `src/tooling/defer` with `ASCIICHAT_DEFER_BUILD_FUZZER=ON`, build
 * `ascii-defer-fuzz`, and run it with a writable copy of `tests/fuzz/defer` as
 * the corpus. Successful transformations are parsed again and must remain valid C.
 */
